[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Memory Management, Garbage Collection, and Object Leaks in Object-Oriented Programming"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding how memory is allocated, utilized, and reclaimed in Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is critical for building high-performance, scalable applications. Poor memory management leads to high resource consumption, sluggish performance, and the dreaded OutOfMemoryError."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Memory Allocation in OOP"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In object-oriented languages like Java, C#, and Python, memory is generally divided into two main regions: the Stack and the Heap. The Stack stores primitive data types and reference variables (pointers) to objects. The Heap stores the actual object instances and their instance variables."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"public class MemoryDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int primitiveValue = 10; // Stored on Stack
        Person person = new Person("Alice"); // Reference on Stack, Object on Heap
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Garbage Collection (GC)"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Garbage Collection is the automated process of identifying and discarding objects that are no longer needed by a program, thereby freeing up heap memory. Most modern garbage collectors use algorithms like Mark-and-Sweep."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mark Phase: The GC traverses the object graph starting from root references (such as local variables and static fields) and marks all reachable objects as "alive"."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sweep Phase: The GC scans the heap and reclaims the memory occupied by unmarked (unreachable) objects."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Object Leaks (Memory Leaks)"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"An object leak occurs when objects are no longer needed by the application logic, but they remain referenced by root pointers, preventing the Garbage Collector from collecting them. Over time, these leaking objects consume available heap space."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"public class LeakExample {
    private static List<Double> cache = new ArrayList<>();

    public void addData() {
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
            cache.add(Math.random());
        }
        // cache is static, so these objects are never garbage collected
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Best Practices for Preventing Memory Leaks"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Nullify references when objects are no longer needed, especially in long-lived singletons or static collections."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use WeakReferences when implementing caches or mapping tables to allow the GC to reclaim entries if memory gets tight."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always unregister event listeners, callbacks, and observers when the observing object is destroyed."},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By mastering memory allocation, understanding garbage collection mechanics, and actively guarding against object leaks, developers can ensure their object-oriented applications remain stable and efficient under heavy workloads."}]
